[
 #:seon.task{:name "the-current-state-block-misleads-and-is-large"
             :kind :function
             :intent "The current-state block (PB-15 dogfood run, lane dogfood-pb15, default, 2026-10-08): a proof shows :green with nothing saying a task whose proofs are all green proves nothing; must-read seon.render.value/render-ai shows :seon.ref/line 1 (wrong); :seon.fn/spec prints as a string instead of data; the block draws a :large-value finding against itself (1,108 tokens). Done when each is fixed at its producer (seon.context/current-state, seon.ref) with a regression per item."
             :must-read #{seon.context/current-state seon.ref/references}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-lost-model-call-is-not-in-the-model-context"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Since CX-1 the model's context is (help), its own model exchanges and the block. A model call lost to a provider failure leaves a call row with a failed attempt and no evaluation, so the next model request does not say the call was lost or why (the system turn's fault read used to carry it). Done when the next model request shows the lost call's error: its exchange, or the block's errors section."
             :must-read #{seon.context/model-context seon.context/current-state}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-message-to-the-agent-is-not-in-the-model-context"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Since CX-1 the model's context is (help), its own model exchanges and the block; a message sent to the agent (the wake's content, root's instructions) reaches the model nowhere until the block shows unread messages (ledger row CS-01c4). Done when the model request after a message carries that message, once, with its identity."
             :must-read #{seon.context/model-context seon.context/current-state}}
 #:seon.task{:name "an-agents-context-omits-the-errors-mcp-shows"
             :kind :function
             :intent "The MCP reply for a branch agent's evaluation carries :errors (its open errors with signature, operation and count); the same agent's next model request carries none (repl-dogfood 2026-09-27, then the old prompt; still true of the model context since CX-1). An error is delivered in the reply of the agent that can act on it, the MCP reply and the next model request reading the same saved observation. Done when the model request shows the same error observation the MCP reply shows."
             :must-read #{seon.context/model-context seon.repl/response}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/repl-dogfood-2026-09-27.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "proofs-of-one-task-allocates-close-to-a-gigabyte"
             :kind :function
             :intent "(`seon.context/proofs` (`seon.db/db`) 'seon.db/the-missing-connection-refusal-has-no-fix-key) at root's SCI REPL allocated 887 MB in 403 ms for one proof (the :large-allocation finding; fanout-3 run, default 324461975, 2026-10-09); the next call for another one-proof task allocated 48 MB in 24 ms. Done when one task's proofs cost in proportion to its proofs, measured with `seon.profile/measure` on a first call."
             :must-read #{seon.context/proofs}}
 #:seon.task{:name "the-block-names-no-namespace"
             :kind :function
             :intent "`seon.context/current-state` carries no namespace, and `seon.repl/response-entries` shows `:ns` only when a form changed it, so after its first call nothing in the regenerated context says where the agent is: chain run 2's agents re-entered their namespace on 13 of 15 and 9 of 14 calls (DYNAMIC-CONTEXT stage 1). Done when the block's turns section holds `:seon.context/namespace`, the namespace the agent's latest evaluation ended in (`seon.turn/previous-ending-namespace`), else its held task's namespace."
             :must-read #{seon.context/current-state seon.turn/previous-ending-namespace}
             :evidence #{"docs/seon/schedule/cards/DYNAMIC-CONTEXT.md"}}

 #:seon.task{:name "one-agents-current-state-costs-hundreds-of-megabytes"
  :kind :function
  :intent "`seon.context/current-state` for one agent costs about 380 ms and 433 MB on default (lane dc-lifecycle, 2026-10-10, HEAD a6cbe4fcf); it runs once per model call to build the block. What the work must do: read the agent's one-hop attachments (task, its references, latest evaluations, proofs) and render them, proportional to what the block shows, not to the program. Profile it with `seon.profile/measure`, name the armed functions that allocate, fix the owner; done when one agent's block is under 50 ms and 50 MB on default."
  :must-read #{seon.context/current-state}
  :evidence #{"docs/seon/plan.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-prove-steps-red-proof-for-its-successor-is-not-a-reached-red"
  :kind :function
  :intent "A Design+Prove step can never end :done. Its work is a red behavior proof tagged with the step that needs it (TASK-CHAIN §3); on its branch that test is red and main does not hold it, so `seon.test/change-reds` lists it, the turn stores it as `:seon.agent/reached-red`, and `seon.context/task-done?` answers false although the step's own proofs are green. Observed on default 2319152dc (chain experiment 1, attempt e95d4e90b70f): its step proof went green at call 5, `:seon.agent/reached-red` held its own new test, and the agent spent calls 6-15 restating that it was done until it ended :capped. Correct: a red test tagged only with tasks other than the held one (here the successor that `:seon.task/needs` the held step) is that task's proof, not a test the change broke; it is neither reached-red nor blocks `seon.context/task-done?`. Proof: `seon.cluster.db-flow-test/a-prove-steps-red-proof-for-its-successor-is-not-a-reached-red` (in the evidence patch until db_flow_test's owner applies it)."
  :must-read #{seon.context/step-status seon.context/task-done? seon.task/obligations seon.task/task-edges}
  :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/chain-experiment-1.md" "docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/chain-experiment-1-proofs.patch"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "reached-reds-are-invisible-to-the-agent"
  :kind :function
  :intent "When a task's proofs are green but `:seon.agent/reached-red` names tests its change broke, `seon.context/task-done?` is false, yet neither `my.task/current` nor `seon.context/current-state` shows those tests, so the agent cannot see why it is not done. Observed on default 2319152dc (chain experiment 1, attempt b83a488f2b98): its proof went green at call 3, `seon.sci.forgive-test/the-reader-reads-a-reply-by-its-line-rules` (8 failures) and `seon.sci.forgive-test/recorded-replies-read-into-the-same-segments` (3) were reached-red, and calls 4-15 repeated (my.task/current) and 'the task is :done' until :capped; a send-back to Design+Prove was the right move and never considered. Correct: `my.task/current` and the block's task section answer the reached reds beside the proofs, each with its failing data, as `seon.context/proofs` does for a red proof."
  :must-read #{my.task/current seon.context/current-state seon.context/task-done? seon.context/proofs}
  :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/chain-experiment-1.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-step-proof-cannot-observe-its-agents-test-evidence"
  :kind :function
  :intent "A step proof that reads test evidence through the program cannot be satisfied: a test member runs on its own branch, and `seon.test.runner/latest-results` reads only runs recorded for the database's own branch, so `seon.context/proofs` inside the member answers :pending for every proof the agent's branch ran red. Observed on default 2319152dc (chain experiment 1, attempt 08d6fe652434): the step proof `(every? #{:red} (map :seon.context/proof-status (:seon.context/proof-list (seon.context/proofs (seon.db/db) 'seon.sci.forgive/trailing-prose-implement))))` stayed red while the same read at the agent's REPL answered [:red]; the agent spent 15 calls and ended :capped. TASK-CHAIN §3b says a step proof runs against the subject's revision and the branch program supplied as explicit test inputs; decide the input (the held step's branch and commit) and supply it to the member, so Prove's proof can assert observed red on the agent's program."
  :must-read #{seon.test.runner/latest-results seon.context/proofs seon.test/run}
  :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/chain-experiment-1.md" "docs/seon/schedule/cards/TASK-CHAIN.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-proof-green-before-the-work-is-not-done"
             :kind :function
             :intent "(hybrid-done-and-merge M1, owner rule 2026-10-12) A step whose proofs were already green at its fork point has nothing to do. `seon.context/step-observation` opens the fork commit (`seon.turn/fork-commit`) with `seon.db/commit-database`, takes each proof's `seon.test.runner/reach-digests` there and reads the runs the branch already holds at those digests with one `seon.test.runner/result-at-reach`: red is fork point true, green false, no run unknown (today's fork point, never green). `seon.context/step-status` answers `:seon.task/status` `:satisfied` for an `:implement` step whose proofs are all green and all known green at the fork point, naming them in `:seon.context/green-before`; `seon.turn/proved-unanswered?` and `seon.turn/next-agent-work` open no model turn for it (`seon.context/task-finished?`). No extra proof run, no admit-run change, nothing stored. Proofs: `seon.test.runner-test/a-result-at-the-base-reach-digest-survives-later-runs`, `seon.context-test/each-step-kind-is-done-by-its-one-rule`, `seon.cluster.db-flow-test/a-task-satisfied-at-the-base-opens-no-model-turn`."
             :must-read #{seon.context/step-status seon.context/step-observation seon.context/step-verdict seon.context/task-finished? seon.turn/fork-commit seon.turn/proved-unanswered? seon.turn/next-agent-work seon.test.runner/result-at-reach seon.test.runner/reach-digests seon.db/commit-database seon.profile/measure}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/hybrid-done-and-merge.md" "docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/m1-notes.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "the-current-state-shows-the-branch-diff-since-its-merge-base"
             :kind :function
             :intent "(hybrid-done-and-merge M14) The branch is the proposal. `seon.context/current-state` carries a derived `:seon.context/changes` (new key in `resources/seon/schemas/seon.context.edn`, never stored): the entities the branch added, changed and removed between its `:seon.task/merge-base` and its head, from `seon.cluster.source/changed-identities` (the hybrid's `seon.program/changed-identities` is the same operation; it is `seon.program/three-way`'s sibling) over program and cluster attributes, grouped by family (functions, tests, schemas, namespaces, task facts, other cluster facts), each entry with the form that digs into it (`my.program/diff`, a pull); `my.program/diff` reads the same base. Red proof: `seon.context-test/the-current-state-shows-the-branch-diff-since-its-merge-base` (after one defn, one deftest and one `my.task/refine!` the block holds `:seon.context/changes` with one entry each under :functions, :tests, :task-facts, each with a readable `:seon.render/form`; an unchanged branch holds no `:seon.context/changes`). Cost: at most 5 ms per eval request on a ten-change branch (0.8 ms warm for the diff). After M3."
             :must-read #{seon.context/current-state seon.cluster.source/changed-identities seon.program/three-way my.program/diff seon.profile/measure}
             :needs #{[:seon.task/sym seon.cluster.squash/a-repeated-auto-merge-adds-nothing]}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/hybrid-done-and-merge.md"}}]
